# Figures and tables: captions, placement, what each carries

Every PDF is a vector file with embedded TrueType (Type 42) fonts, **cropped to 5.46 in (393 pt) wide or less**:
place each at `\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{...}` in the single column (text width 5.5 in) or smaller only if you accept a
proportional loss of type size (smallest type is 8 pt at natural size; any scale below 1.0 breaks the 8 pt minimum). The numbers
plotted are in the CSV next to each PDF. Each generator is `figures/gen/<name>.py RESULTS_DIR OUT_DIR` (see `REPRODUCE.md` §9).
Names of results are the analysis role's; no number is computed in a generator (ratios, intervals, verdicts are read from `results/`).

Numbering: the headings below carry the number the paper gives each item (the file names keep the generators' names, which are older).
Figure 1 `fig3_bias_sd`, 2 `fig1_regime_map`, 3 `fig5_tiers`, 4 `fig9_coverage`, 5 `fig7_diagnostic`, 6 `figA2_variance`, 7 `figA4_p`,
8 `figA8_robust`, 9 `figA9_orfit`, 10 `figP6_interval`, 11 `fig6_cluster`; Table 1 `t3_regime`, 2 `t2_acceptance`, 3 `t1_mechanisms`, 4 `t1_calibration_full`.

Settings common to the main figures unless stated: Bernoulli design, treatment probability p = 1/2, noise multiple sigma = 0.5, i.i.d. baseline of SD 1,
SBM n ~ 3000, mean degree d = 20 ("core cell"); 10 graph draws x 300 assignments per synthetic cell, 1 graph x 3000 assignments on the real graphs.

## Paper Figure 2 (C4, headline) `figures/fig1_regime_map.pdf`, data `fig1_regime_map_A.csv`, `fig1_regime_map_B.csv`
Carries: where regression beats DM, as a function of spillover share s and mechanism, and the break-even share s* as d grows.
Caption draft: **Where the regression estimator beats difference in means (DM).** (A) log2 of RMSE(Chin regression)/RMSE(DM): blue = regression better, orange = DM better;
core SBM (n ~ 3000, d = 20, p = 1/2, sigma = 0.5) over the spillover share s (column s = 0 is the no-interference null) and mechanisms (a)-(f); then the two real graphs
(Stanford3, ego-Facebook, thinned to mean degree 20) at s = 0.25, 0.5, 0.75. A dot in a cell marks a ratio whose paired 95% interval contains 0. (B) Break-even share s*
(smallest s from which the regression has the lower RMSE) against mean degree d, one line per mechanism, with 95% intervals (paired two-level bootstrap), for sigma = 0.25, 0.5, 1.
At d = 5 the regression wins at the lowest grid share (s = 0.1) for sigma <= 0.5, so s* is only bounded there (arrow, s* <= 0.1). Triangle at 1: mechanism (d) at d = 20, where DM wins at every s.
Caveats to print or footnote: mechanism (e) at s = 0.9 is truncated at K = 30 steps; the sign-error rate is ~0 at sigma <= 1 in these cells and is therefore not drawn (sigma = 2, 4 are extensions).
What the reader should see: orange only at small s (s = 0 and 0.1 for every mechanism, also s = 0.2 for (e) and (f)) and for mechanism (d), which is orange at every s on the synthetic d = 20 graph but blue on both real graphs; s* rises with d.

## Paper Figure 1 (C2, C3) `figures/fig3_bias_sd.pdf`, data `fig3_bias_sd.csv`
Carries: bias and SD of the regression move differently with d (row A) and with p (row B), mechanism-specific; DM bias dashed.
Caption draft: **Bias and SD of the Chin regression (units of tau = 1) against mean degree d (row A, p = 1/2) and treated share p (row B, d = 20).** Core SBM, s = 0.5, sigma = 0.5.
Columns group the mechanisms by the specification error of the regression: (a) fraction, (b) count; (c) saturating; (d) threshold; (e) multi-hop, (f) interaction. Orange filled markers: bias; blue open markers: SD; bars: 95% Monte Carlo intervals; dashed: DM bias.
Scope note for the caption (outline rev 18): the SBM has near-Poisson degrees; the fall of the (b) bias with d (0.115, 0.025, 0.007) holds for such degrees, not for the BA graph or the real graphs, where the degree dispersion does not fall with d.
Note for the text: the p grid is (0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9) for every mechanism and degree except (c) at d = 20, which also has p = 0.35-0.65 in steps of 0.05 (runs `pfine_SBM_n3000_d20_p*`; the figure draws these points, so its zero crossing, 0.397 [0.393, 0.401] by interpolation between measured neighbours, is bracketed by measurements); elsewhere a zero crossing is interpolated, not measured (no minimiser is marked).

## Paper Figure 3 (C4, tiers) `figures/fig5_tiers.pdf`, data `fig5_tiers.csv`
Caption draft: **RMSE relative to DM by information tier at the core cell.** Dot = RMSE(estimator)/RMSE(DM) (log axis; < 1 better than DM), bar = 95% paired bootstrap interval, one dot per mechanism (colour and marker, as in the legend).
Tier G (graph known, no mechanism parameter), Tier A (data-adaptive; the adaptive-threshold Hajek is the simplified version listed in Appendix X), Tier O (oracle form; "fitted rho" is the one-parameter diffusion/saturation/threshold form with its shape parameter profiled on a grid, not message passing).
Filled symbols: the 200-draw set on which every Tier G and Tier A estimator is paired; open symbols: HT/Hajek and the Tier O rows, from the 3000-draw set of the same cell. Comparisons are meant within a tier; the cross-tier view is one cell.
Note on the fitted-form row (analysis, `analysis/or_fit_check.py`): it is worse than DM at (c) and (e) for d >= 20 by a real property, not a fitting defect: the profile of the sum of squares over the shape parameter is nearly flat on the observed support (median relative range 0.05% at d = 20 for (c)), the minimiser lands at an end of the grid in most draws, and the corner contrast extrapolated from a wrong shape is far off; at d = 5 the fitted form equals the known form. A caption sentence: "fitted rho: shape parameter profiled over a grid; the profile is flat on the observed support, so the fit is unstable at d >= 20".
Note: the fitted-form row has no (a), (b), (f): those forms have no shape parameter to fit. For (d) the fitted form equals the true-parameter form (the threshold grid contains 0.6).

## Paper Figure 11 (C5) `figures/fig6_cluster.pdf`, data `fig6_cluster.csv`
Caption draft: **Graph-cluster randomisation.** RMSE (units of tau) of the Chin regression under the cluster design (filled blue), DM under the cluster design (black square) and the Chin regression under Bernoulli randomisation on the same graph (open diamond), with 95% Monte Carlo intervals;
mechanisms (a), (c), (d), s = 0.5, sigma = 0.5, p = 1/2, d = 20 unless stated. Rows are cluster designs ordered by the modularity Q of the partition (printed at the row label). Louvain resolution gamma = 1 and 3 (SBM: the planted partition, d = 5, 20, 80).
Louvain at gamma = 0.3 returns one cluster on every synthetic graph tried, so no design exists there (`cluster_quality.csv`); it is shown for the real graphs only. The weak-mixing SBM has no Bernoulli arm. Estimates with an undefined exposure group (Hajek) are not plotted.
What the reader should see (from `fig6_cluster.csv`): for (d) cluster DM has the lower RMSE than the cluster regression in every row except the Stanford3 rows at gamma = 1 and 3; for (a) and (c) the cluster regression is lower in almost every row (exceptions: (a) at SBM d = 80, 0.174 against 0.162 for DM, and the Stanford3 gamma = 0.3 row).

